./docs/codex-testing-suite-design-6-21-26.md:327:- “Every request ends terminal within timeout” only works if the app has a crash supervisor that converts task exceptions into durable terminal state. Tests alone cannot infer a killed background task cleanly.
./docs/codex-testing-suite-design-6-21-26.md:607:2. A build run silently hung: status stayed RUNNING forever with the model idle and no UI error. Root cause: a sandbox file read raised SandboxError (a renamed file), which subclasses Exception (NOT the native OSError/FileNotFoundError hierarchy). ~8 call sites across the loop already had `except (FileNotFoundError, OSError, ...)` handlers intending to tolerate missing files — none catch SandboxError, so against the real sandbox backend they are all dead code; the exception escaped, killed the loop task, and nothing converted the crash into a terminal ERROR status.
./docs/codex-testing-suite-design-6-21-26.md:615:- Browser automation (Playwright + headless Chromium) was attempted repeatedly to capture rendered output and failed repeatedly: the workstation caps each command in a memory cgroup (~16 GB) and Chromium gets OOM-killed on the heavy SPA; also frequent timing flakiness (TargetClosedError, font-load races, networkidle vs domcontentloaded).
